  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The strength of ligands discussed in spectrochemical series.</p><p>According to the limitations of VBT,</p><p>It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic or kinetic stabilities of coordination compounds.</p><p> Hybridisation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>]</mo><msup><mo> </mo><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>dsp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.</p><p>The outer electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> ion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></math>.</p><p>It is a square planar complex. Cyanide ion is a strong field ligand, thus all electrons will be paired.</p><p>One <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>, one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo> </mo></math>orbitals undergo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>dsp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> hybridization, four equivalent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>dsp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> hybrid orbitals which are occupied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> electron pairs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> cyanide ligands.</p><p>This forms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ni</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>]</mo><msup><mo> </mo><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cis</mi><mo>-</mo><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>PtCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>]</mo><msup><mo> </mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> complex exhibits optical isomerism, hence, it has two isomers.</p><p>Hence statement B and C are correct.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
